Metal: use validated timeouts for blocking completion waits

Replace command-buffer status polling with completion-handler blocking waits while
keeping timeout failures sticky. Shared-event waits now use an absolute steady-
clock deadline and clamp every Metal wait to the remaining API-granularity
budget.

Use one validated timeout accessor for both paths. Signed, malformed, zero,
overflowing, and policy-excess values are rejected with a warning and fall back
to the 120000 ms default; the policy maximum is 24 hours.

Add parser and cached-accessor boundary tests, plus deterministic failure-sink
tests proving timeout and command-buffer error results remain sticky for later
waits. An Apple-only integration test drives an unsignaled shared event through
the production event-wait implementation, checks the retained registered error,
and exercises the real Metal command-buffer error-status mapping.


